    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Nanak was born into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> Khatri family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, worked in the state stores at Sultanpur, and, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> belief, received a divine call in about 1496 after vanishing into the Bein river.</li><li>He taught one God, Ik Onkar, the equality of people regardless of gender, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> or religion, and a householder's life of honest work, sharing and remembrance of the divine name.</li><li>He settled at Kartarpur, where his community practised communal eating and singing, and he passed the guruship to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-angad">Bhai Lehna</a>, who became Guru Angad, not to his sons.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-arjan">Guru Arjan</a> included 974 hymns by Nanak in the Ādi Granth in 1604, and the Japji forms the opening of the work.</p></aside>
    </section>

