    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1469</span><span class="rf-text">Nanak is born at Rāi Bhoi Dī Talvaṇḍī, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nankana-sahib">Nankana Sahib</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1487</span><span class="rf-text">He marries Sulakhani at Batala</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1496</span><span class="rf-text">He disappears into the Bein river and returns after three or four days, saying that there is no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and no Muslim</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1508</span><span class="rf-text">He visits the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sylhet">Sylhet</a> region in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, according to modern accounts</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1520s</span><span class="rf-text">Around the age of 55 he settles at Kartarpur on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi">Ravi</a>, where the Nanak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panth">Panth</a> takes shape</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1539</span><span class="rf-text">He names <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-angad">Bhai Lehna</a> as Guru Angad and dies at Kartarpur on 22 September</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1604</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-arjan">Guru Arjan</a> includes 974 of his hymns in the Ādi Granth</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nankana-sahib"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nankana-sahib.jpg" alt="Nankana Sahib" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nankana-sahib">Nankana Sahib</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Entrance_of_Janam_Asthan-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Shaguftakarim, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Nanak was born on 15 April 1469 at Rāi Bhoi Dī Talvaṇḍī, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nankana-sahib">Nankana Sahib</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. He was a Bedi Khatri. A second tradition places his birth in November, in the month of Kārtik, and the question is still debated: most janamsakhis, the traditional biographies, say Baisakh, and the festival was held in April as late as 1815.</p><p>His father, Kalyan Chand Das Bedi, called Mehta Kalu, was the village <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/village-accountant">patwari</a>, an accountant of crop revenue, who worked for Rai Bullar, a Bhatti <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> who had converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. His mother was Tripta, and his elder sister Nanaki was four or five years older. His childhood friend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhai-mardana">Mardana</a> was a Muslim rababi.</p><p>According to the janamsakhis, he studied accounting and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devanagari">Devanagari</a> script, then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedas">Vedas</a>, and at nine went to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuttab">maktab</a> to learn Persian and Arabic. His father hoped he would take up accounting or trade, but he spent time with sadhus and sufis. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> lore says he refused the janaeu thread at his initiation, which began his break with orthodox traditions. Sources that were written to honour him include many miracles, such as a shadow from a tree or a cobra shading his sleeping head. Scholars treat these as hagiography.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Guru Nanak&#8221; cites where it covers Birth, Family and early life, Spiritual rebirth:</p><ul><li>Arvind-Pal Singh Mandair, Sikhism: A Guide for the Perplexed (Bloomsbury Academic, 2013)</li><li>Nikky-Guninder Kaur Singh, Sikhism: An Introduction (I. B. Tauris, 2011)</li><li>Hari Ram Gupta, History of the Sikhs: The Sikh Gurus, 1469-1708 (Munshiram Manoharlal, 1984)</li><li>Kapur Singh, Guru Nanak's Life and Thought (Guru Nanak Dev University, 2001)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bhai-mardana"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhai-mardana.jpg" alt="Bhai Mardana" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bhai-mardana">Bhai Mardana</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detail_of_Bhai_Mardana_from_a_Janamsakhi_series_of_painting.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist. Published in sourced book. Page photographed by me (Wikimedia Commons user ThethPunjabi)., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Nanak married Sulakhani, daughter of Mūl Chand, on 24 September 1487 at Batala. They had two sons, Sri Chand, born in 1494, and Lakhmi Chand, born in 1496 or 1497. Through his sister's husband, Jai Ram, he found work at the modikhana, a store for revenues, in the service of Daulat Khan, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> governor, at Sultanpur. He worked there as custodian of the stores for about twelve years, singing hymns with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhai-mardana">Mardana</a> at dawn and night. Scholars note that his hymns' references to government probably come from this period.</p><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> belief, he disappeared while bathing in the Bein river. His clothes were found, and people assumed he had drowned. He returned on the third or fourth day, silent, and then said: 'There is no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, there is no Muslim.' The Janamsakhi literature says that during his absence he was brought to the court of God, was given amrit and a robe of honour, and was told to spread naam simran, dan, isnan and seva. His reply to the test of how to praise the divine name was the opening of the Japji. Sikhs date this event to about the age of 33, while Kapur Singh dates it to about 1496, when he was 27. The first to accept that he had reached enlightenment was his sister Nanaki.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/babur"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/babur.jpg" alt="Babur" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Humayun_and_Babur_(Late_Shah_Jahan_Album)_Babur_detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">c.1650, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the event at Sultanpur he left government service and began a spiritual journey. The Janamsakhis describe lengthy tours, the udasis, across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> and beyond, usually four: east to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>; south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>; north to Ladakh and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>; and west to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhai-mardana">Mardana</a> accompanied him on the eastern and western tours. He wore clothing associated with both fakirs and sadhus, held discourses with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Muslim figures, and visited temples, mosques and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> centres. He is said to have witnessed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>'s invasion at Saidpur, which four hymns, the Babur Vani, record.</p><p>At about fifty he gathered a following, the Nanak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panth">Panth</a>, and at about fifty-five he settled at Kartarpur, on land a follower donated beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi">Ravi</a>. He lived as a householder and taught honest work, devotion and sharing. The community practised communal eating, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/langar-sikhism">langar</a>, selfless service, and singing together at dawn. He taught the equality of humankind, rejected the divisions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a>, said that women were not impure or inferior, and criticised the rulers of his day for greed and cruelty. The Japji was finalised there and put into the daily recitation.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Guru Nanak&#8221; cites where it covers Teachings, Artwork:</p><ul><li>Arvind-Pal Singh Mandair, Sikhism: A Guide for the Perplexed (Bloomsbury Academic, 2013)</li><li>William Owen Cole, The Sikhs: Their Religious Beliefs and Practices (Sussex Academic Press, 1995)</li><li>Nikky-Guninder Kaur Singh, Sikhism: An Introduction (I. B. Tauris, 2011)</li><li>Arvind-Pal Singh Mandair, Religion and the Specter of the West – Sikhism, India, Postcoloniality and the Politics of Translation (Columbia University Press, 2009)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The sources record resistance and disagreement more than organised persecution. Nanak's father is said to have struck him for spending money on feeding hungry sadhus, and his family thought his withdrawal showed a lack of ambition and, at one point, possession. After his saying that there was no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and no Muslim, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qazi">Qazi</a> complained to Daulat Khan Lodhi, and Nanak was summoned to Sultanpur.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> tradition holds that his rejection of rites such as the janaeu thread began his break with Vedic and Hindu orthodoxy, and he denied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> divisions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varna</a>. He also criticised the prevailing political regimes for greed and cruelty.</p><p>Modern scholarship disputes much of the life. The janamsakhis, the Bala, Miharban, Adi and Puratan traditions, were written by later followers with no intention of exact history, and Callewaert and Snell find that early Sikh texts do not hold the travel stories. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> stone with an inscription in a Turkish script is read as 1511 to 1512 by some and as 1521 to 1522 by others, and Western scholars argue that it is from the 19th century. No Middle Eastern record mentions a visit. The Bala janamsakhi's October birth date was written by the Handalis, a sect that wished to depict its own founder as superior to Nanak.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/guru-angad"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guru-angad.jpg" alt="Guru Angad" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/guru-angad">Guru Angad</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guru_Angad_miniature_painting.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">possibly the family workshop (or atelier) of Nainsukh of Guler, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Nanak examined his sons and, according to Bhai Gurdas, both failed. Sri Chand was an ascetic, a lifestyle Nanak had reprimanded, and Lakhmi Das was not interested in religion. Nanak appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-angad">Bhai Lehna</a> of Khadur as his successor and renamed him Guru Angad, 'one's very own'. Lehna had been a worshipper of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a>. The ceremony placed a coconut and five paisse before him, with Nanak bowing toward him. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> hold that the guruship passed from Nanak to Angad as one light passing between two bodies. Mandair says that by choosing a successor Nanak meant his work to continue, which helped the early community survive as a distinct group.</p><p>Nanak died on 22 September 1539 at Kartarpur, at the age of 70. According to the Puratan Janamsakhi, his Muslim and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> followers quarrelled over what to do with his body. When they pulled at the sheet covering him, they found only flowers. The Muslims and Hindus each took flowers and buried or cremated them by their own customs. Scholars note that such stories resemble miracle stories told of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> pirs.</p><p>After his death Guru Angad moved the community to Khadur, as Nanak's sons claimed the property at Kartarpur. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi">Ravi</a> later washed away his mausoleum, and his son reburied the urn with his ashes on the left bank, where Dera Baba Nanak grew up.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Guru Nanak&#8221; cites where it covers Death and succession:</p><ul><li>Arvind-Pal Singh Mandair, Sikhism: A Guide for the Perplexed (Bloomsbury Academic, 2013)</li><li>Nikky-Guninder Kaur Singh, Sikhism: An Introduction (I. B. Tauris, 2011)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib.jpg" alt="Guru Granth Sahib" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib">Guru Granth Sahib</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sri_Guru_Granth_Sahib_Nishan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> regard Nanak as the founder of Sikhism and the first of ten gurus, and hold that his sanctity and authority passed to each of the nine who followed. His 974 hymns stand in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib">Guru Granth Sahib</a>, which Sikhs revere as the final and perpetual guru. They include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japji-sahib">Japji Sahib</a>, the Asa di Var and the Sidh Gosht. He wrote in a composite vernacular, Sant Bhasha, in simple metre and with comparisons, and he called himself a poet. His birthday is celebrated as Guru Nanak Gurpurab, held on the full moon of Kārtik since at least 1868.</p><p>Sikhs understand his teaching as naam japo, kirat karo and vand chhako, which are remembering God's name, honest work and sharing. Nikky-Guninder Kaur Singh sees the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalsa">Khalsa</a> in 1699 as an attempt to return to the equality of Kartarpur.</p><p>Scholars disagree about what produced his teaching. Earlier writers called it a mixture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Recent work sets it among the nirguni Sant and Bhakti movements, with some Islamic influence, and James R. Lewis criticises the label 'syncretic'. Sikhs differ on whether he was a guru and not a prophet, or an incarnation of God.</p><p>Other traditions claim him: the Bahá'í Faith calls him a saint of the highest order, many Hindus follow Nanakpanthi teachings, the Ahmadiyya Muslim Community holds that he was a Muslim saint, and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> have regarded him as an incarnation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padmasambhava">Padmasambhava</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Guru Nanak&#8221; cites where it covers Influences, Legacy:</p><ul><li>Louise E. Fenech, The Oxford Handbook of Sikh Studies (Oxford University Press, 2014)</li><li>David N. Lorenzen, Bhakti Religion in North India: Community Identity and Political Action (State University of New York Press, 1995)</li><li>Nikky-Guninder Kaur Singh, Sikhism: An Introduction (I. B. Tauris, 2011)</li><li>Christopher Shackle, Teachings of the Sikh Gurus: Selections from the Sikh Scriptures (Routledge, 2013)</li></ul></details><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/japji-sahib">Japji Sahib<span>date unknown</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Guru Nanak, born in 1469 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, taught devotion to one God and the equality of people, composed 974 hymns kept in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib">Guru Granth Sahib</a>, and founded a community at Kartarpur. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> regard him as the first of their ten gurus.</em></p>

